{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l4taqnnd77ty72msvqamra4dwudablywcjceymufhfs4wshrs72q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01QWBFZmbzTFJKxeSWUGhLaJ","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"general-purpose","description":"Independent C vs Rust semantic audit","model":"sonnet","prompt":"You are auditing a C-to-Rust translation for EXACT behavioral equivalence. Do NOT modify any files. This is a read-and-report task only.\n\nWorking directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ghXTe7\n\nFiles:\n- C ground truth (NEVER modify, read only): c_src/src/main.c  (85 lines)\n- Rust translation: translation/src/main.rs\n\nBoth are already built:\n- C binary:    /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ghXTe7/c_src/build/driver\n- Rust binary: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ghXTe7/translation/target/release/driver\n\nThe program takes 1 to 3 command line arguments: <string> [start] [stop] and prints\nthe byte substring [start, stop) of <string>. It does NOT read stdin.\n\nYOUR JOB: find any input for which the two binaries differ in (a) stdout bytes,\n(b) stderr bytes, or (c) exit status. Read both source files closely first, then\nattack the differences you suspect empirically.\n\nAreas already verified as matching (do not re-verify, look for NEW issues):\n- argc<2 and argc>4 usage error; single arg; empty string\n- non-integer 2nd arg (\"Second argument must be an integer!\", no newline)\n- negative start/stop becoming huge unsigned via int-vs-size_t comparison\n- long->int truncation (e.g. 4294967296 -> 0) and strtol LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN saturation\n- the dead \"Third argument must be an integer!\" branch (stale `end` pointer)\n- stop>len and stop<=start checks and their ORDER\n- non-UTF-8 argv bytes; multi-byte UTF-8 byte-slicing\n- SIGPIPE on broken stdout; /dev/full; closed stdout; locale env vars\n\nFOCUS your hunt on things NOT in that list, especially:\n1. glibc strtol() exact acceptance grammar vs the hand-written c_strtol_base10()\n   in the Rust. Try: \"+ 5\", \"--5\", \"-+5\", \"++5\", \" -  5\", extremely long digit\n   strings (5000 digits), 10000 leading zeros then a digit, \"0000000000000000005\",\n   embedded NUL is impossible, \"5\\t\", \"\\t\\t\\t\\t5\", strings of only signs, \"٥\"\n   (Arabic-Indic), full-width digits, \"5e\", \"0X5\", \"5.9\", \"InF\", \"nan\".\n2. Values right at every boundary: len, len+1, INT_MAX, INT_MIN, UINT_MAX,\n   LONG_MAX, LONG_MIN, 2^31, 2^32, 2^63, 2^64, and those values +/- 1, in BOTH\n   the start and stop positions.\n3. Any case where the Rust could panic (index out of range, slice out of bounds,\n   integer overflow in debug/release, unwrap) but the C would not. Note the Rust\n   has panic=\"abort\". Look hard at the final slice `&argv[1][offset..]` and the\n   `stop.wrapping_sub(start)` precision.\n4. Whether `%.*s` with a NEGATIVE precision is reachable, and if so whether the\n   Rust matches C (C treats negative precision as if omitted).\n\nMethod: write a Python or bash fuzz/compare harness in $TMPDIR (NOT in the repo)\nthat runs both binaries with identical argv and compares stdout, stderr and exit\ncode byte-for-byte. Use subprocess with a list of args so no shell quoting\ninterferes. Test tens of thousands of cases including systematic boundary sweeps.\nNote: a 0 byte cannot be passed in argv at all, and total argv size is limited to\nabout 2MB (ARG_MAX), so keep individual args under ~100KB.\n\nREPORT BACK: the exact argv vectors of any mismatch found with the C output vs\nRust output, or a clear statement that you found zero mismatches and how many\ncases you tried. Be precise and do not speculate beyond what you tested."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":516,"cache_read_input_tokens":73479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":516,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"out