{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ifeehde6ewubs6z2o46knudrpekvvrdnvqydbgfbem2kkz3uxv2a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete. Here's what I found and fixed.\n\n## Phase A — both build\n- **C:** `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- **Rust:** `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`\n\nBoth built clean, no warnings. The Rust compiled on the first try — no compile errors to fix.\n\n## Phases B & C — two real mismatches found\n\nBoth were in **process-level behavior**, not string logic. The parsing, the `int`-vs-`size_t` quirks, the truncation and the formatting were already correct.\n\n**1. Broken stdout pipe: Rust survived where C dies.** `driver hello | true` — the C is killed by SIGPIPE (signal 13); the Rust exited 0. Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`, so the write returned `EPIPE`, which the code (correctly, mirroring C never checking `printf`) discards. This affected **every** input, since all four branches write to stdout.\n\n**2. My first fix was itself wrong.** Forcing `SIG_DFL` broke the mirror case: when the *parent* already ignores SIGPIPE, C inherits `SIG_IGN` and *survives*, but the Rust now died. C doesn't *set* its disposition, it *inherits* it. Real fix: capture the inherited value from an ELF `.init_array` constructor (which runs before `std::rt::init`) and restore exactly that. Both directions now agree.\n\nI verified the regression tests aren't vacuous — commenting out the restore makes them fail with `C=(None, Some(13)) RUST=(Some(0), None)`.\n\nTwo things worth flagging as **deliberately preserved**, since they look like bugs:\n- The `if (end == argv[3])` check on line 63 tests the *stale* `end` from parsing `argv[2]` (`strtol` got a NULL endptr). I confirmed with a throwaway probe that `end` is always `argv[3]-1` or earlier, so **\"Third argument must be an integer!\" is unreachable** — `driver hello 0 abc` silently gets `stop=0` and reports `stop must come after start!` instead.\n- `start > len` compares `int` against `size_t`, so every negative index becomes huge and reports \"off the end\". `driver hello 4294967296` truncates to 0 and happily prints `hello`.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n- Both build with no errors ✅\n- `cargo test`: **22 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** ✅ (no `#[ignore]`, nothing skipped)\n- `c_src/` unmodified — checksums `e9e03a59…`/`4fdd0b4c…` unchanged, source mtimes still the original checkout time; only a `build/` artifact dir was added, and the test harness falls back to an *out-of-tree* CMake build so it never writes into `c_src/` ✅\n- ~24,500 additional ad-hoc cases compared (strtol grammar, every numeric boundary in both positions, exhaustive start×stop grids, non-UTF-8 argv, 120 KB strings) — zero mismatches ✅\n\n`translation/ERRORS.md` records both mismatches, their causes, the fixes, the preserved quirks, and a branch-by-branch table of the C source mapped to the input that reaches it.\n\nTwo caveats on my own work: the test-suite `long_input_windows` case initially failed because I put a NUL byte in argv, which `execve` can't carry — that was my test bug, not a translation bug, and it's noted in ERRORS.md. And I dispatched a background sub-agent for an independent audit but `SendMessage` is disabled in this session, so I could never retrieve its report; rather than rely on it, I ran its assigned checks (strtol grammar + boundary sweeps, 5,751 cases) myself."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2848,"cache_read_input_tokens":117458,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2848,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3973024c-41c0-4ae5-81ce-f1cb3e2cdf95","uuid":"d4845a21-622d-40b8-8d